| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 2026-11-03 | Copy webproai's rat-stack skeleton (root config, `capability`, `http-transport`, `html-reader`, `scan-store` patterns, `cli`, `scanner`, `infra`, `web`) into this repo under the `@eaa-check` scope, then cut what the EAA checker does not use. | Brief asks for the same stack and reuse; copying a working skeleton is the fastest path to a green end-to-end slice. webproai is not modified. |
| 2 | 2026-11-03 | The facts ledger is Markdown with a fixed per-fact bullet format (`research/facts/README.md`) that tests parse. Product data references fact IDs; a test fails if any referenced fact is not `verified`. | Makes "every claim is sourced" enforceable by the fence instead of by discipline. |
| 3 | 2026-11-03 | Research split: one agent for the directive + EN 301 549 + WCAG, three agents for 9 member states each, one verifier that sets `verified`/`rejected`. Researchers never set status. | Brief's suggested split within ~6 concurrent agents; independent verification. |
| 4 | 2026-11-03 | English UI first. Proposal for Vojta: Czech second (studio9's home market), other languages only on demand. | Brief asks to record it; Czech is the one market studio9 already sells in. |
| 5 | 2026-11-03 | The report lists findings with their WCAG SC + EN 301 549 clause, and a fixed statement that automated checks find only part of the issues and that a manual audit is needed. It never uses the words "compliant" or "compliance score". | Brief rule; avoids a false legal claim. |
| 6 | 2026-11-03 | Built on rat-stack exactly like webproai: same template lineage (joelhooks/rat-stack `e2616fb` via webproai's cut), same fence (Effect language service via tsgo, typed lint plugins, anti-slop, lefthook, no-verify blocks, CI), same workspace layout and `.brain/` shape. | Vojta, via orchestrator, 2026-11-03: "It should be very similar to webproai in structure." |
| 7 | 2026-11-03 | Rendered-DOM checks: axe-core on Cloudflare Browser Run, as a later layer, one Workflow task per scan, under a daily browser-time budget that stays inside Workers Paid's included 10 hours/month. Static checks remain the baseline. | See `.brain/resources/axe-on-browser-run.svx`. Confirmed by Vojta, 2026-11-03 ("confirming the axe code decision"). |
| 8 | 2026-11-03 | Map checks to both EN 301 549 V3.2.1 (WCAG 2.1) and V4.1.1 (2026-09, WCAG 2.2); drop SC 4.1.1 checks (removed in WCAG 2.2). Which version the report leads with follows the OJ citation under 2019/882, from the ledger. | research-eu found V4.1.1 published 2026-09; the brief's "WCAG 2.1 AA" premise may be outdated. |
| 9 | 2026-11-03 | Legal baseline: if the ledger shows EN 301 549 V4.1.1 cited in the OJ as harmonised under 2019/882, it leads; if not cited or unknown, the report treats V3.2.1 / WCAG 2.1 AA as the baseline and labels WCAG 2.2-only findings "upcoming (EN 301 549 V4.1.1)". The report calls the baseline the "reference standard" and claims OJ citation under the EAA only if a verified fact says so (V3.2.1's OJ citation may be under the Web Accessibility Directive 2016/2102, not the EAA). | Orchestrator, 2026-11-03; the last clause is the lead's, to avoid an unsourced presumption-of-conformity claim. |
| 10 | 2026-11-03 | Product wording: the legal requirement is the EAA's Annex I accessibility requirements. EN 301 549 V3.2.1 / WCAG 2.1 AA is presented as the "reference benchmark", never as what the EAA legally requires. EU-HS-1 reads "no EN 301 549 version found cited in the OJ under 2019/882 as of 2026-10-30". | Orchestrator, 2026-11-03: V3.2.1 is cited under 2016/2102 (Implementing Decision (EU) 2021/1339); EAA harmonised standards pending under standardisation request M/587. research-eu sources both. |
| 11 | 2026-11-03 | Bot walls are the main product risk: 9 of 16 large EU shops refused a plain fetch (`.brain/resources/bot-wall-block-rate.svx`). No evasion. A refused scan gets a clear "this site blocked automated checks, nothing was tested" report with the CTA for a manual review. Browser Run's block rate on the same list is measured once the axe layer runs. | Orchestrator spot-check, 2026-11-03; measured by builder-core. |
| 12 | 2026-11-03 | The report leads with coverage (N checks touch M of 50 WCAG 2.1 A/AA SCs, computed from verified facts) and uses "no issue found" for passes, never ticks or "passed". | A page with every check passing must not read as a clean bill of health. |
| 13 | 2026-11-03 | From 20:55 the two builders commit and push their own work whenever the whole-tree gate is green, instead of the lead committing. | Two agents in one working tree with a whole-tree pre-commit gate left few green moments for the lead to commit in; the hook still guards every commit. |
| 14 | 2026-11-03 | Contact path: a form under every settled report stores the message in D1 (daily cap). No email sending and no Turnstile yet. | Gives the CTA a target tonight with no new service; email (Cloudflare Email, as webproai) and Turnstile need Vojta's addresses and site keys. |
| 15 | 2026-11-03 | Aim for broad, accurate coverage: every WCAG 2.1 A/AA SC (and the 2.2 additions) gets a testing method: static, rendered-deterministic (Browser Run session: axe plus our own reflow, text spacing, focus visible, target size probes), model-judged with TypeSafe Jev (pinned `jev-1.14.0`, typed Noul/Choice/Score answers, threshold in code), or manual only. Model-judged findings are shown apart, labelled as judged by a model with its confidence, and never mixed into deterministic counts. Without `TYPESAFE_API_KEY` the judged step is skipped and the report says so. | Vojta, 2026-11-03: "getting as close as we can to state where we are able to test almost everything PRETTY accurately ... we can use jev from typesafe for some decisions". Amends the brief's "checks are deterministic" for a separate, labelled class of findings. Jev: $0.042 per Mtok input, output free (docs.typesafe.ai/models). Images are a "danger zone" feature. |
| 16 | 2026-11-03 | Every issue gets a "Copy fix prompt" button: a deterministic prompt for a coding agent built from the check, the failing samples, the URL and the SC/clause facts, as webproai does with its fix prompts. | Vojta, 2026-11-03. |
| 17 | 2026-11-03 | UI work loads the Foldkit skills (`foldkit-skills:foldkit`, then `foldkit-skills:audit-program` before calling it done). | Vojta, 2026-11-03. |
| 18 | 2026-11-03 | Jev is called through Cloudflare: Workers AI binding with model `typesafe/jev-1.14.0` via AI Gateway, no TypeSafe key. Cloudflare's `@cf/cloudflare/clef` (same System One API, Apache 2.0 weights) is the recorded fallback. | Vojta, 2026-11-03: "ideally we would use it via cloudflare". Cloudflare docs list `typesafe/jev-1.14.0` for the Workers AI binding and AI Gateway. |
| 19 | 2026-11-03 | Judged checks: the model id must be confirmed callable in this account before it is built on (orchestrator found no jev/typesafe in the account's catalog search); fallback `@cf/cloudflare/clef`. Judged findings never feed the issue count and are never presented as facts. Model usage is capped per day within Workers AI's free daily allocation by a budget row in scan-store; when spent, the judged step is skipped with a reason. | Orchestrator, 2026-11-03; keeps the no-new-paid-services limit. |
| 20 | 2026-11-03 | The rendered check also runs when the static fetch was refused (within the browser budget). Browser Run is an honest Cloudflare browser; nothing is disguised, so this is not evasion. If it is refused too, the report says the site blocked automated checks. | builder-cloud measured that Cloudflare egress changes who blocks a plain fetch (zalando, fnac, elcorteingles refused from Cloudflare; mediamarkt, alza passed), and Browser Run was blocked on 0 of the 6 shops it opened. |
| 21 | 2026-11-03 | Judged observations run on `@cf/cloudflare/clef` (Workers AI, inside the free daily allocation, `JudgeBudget` in scan-store). Jev stays the recorded swap-in, one constant, once AI Gateway credits or BYOK are approved. | builder-judge's real call: `typesafe/jev-1.14.0` returned 402 (unified billing, a paid service needing sign-off); Clef answered all three question types correctly. |
| 22 | 2026-11-03 | The daily browser budget is a per-stage setting: production about 18 minutes a day (inside the included 10 browser hours a month), stage dev 90 minutes a day for QA. | Block-rate runs spent the day's budget by 21:30 CET; billing is monthly, and day 3 of the month leaves ample included hours. |
| 23 | 2026-11-03 | Judged observations shown only for checks with precision >= 0.9 on real pages: link purpose (13/14 in QA) and page language. Headings/labels (1/6, wrong on Czech headings) and alt describes (0/1) are hidden until a real labelled sample says otherwise. axe `label-content-name-mismatch` is dropped (about 1 true in 45). | builder-core's accuracy QA on 21 sites (`docs/accuracy-qa.md`); synthetic calibration overstated precision. |
| 24 | 2026-11-03 | Deadline moved to ~01:40 CET; Vojta: "all decisions on you". | Vojta, 21:44. |
| 25 | 2026-11-04 | Fail-only verdict. When a deterministic check (static or rendered, never judged) fails, the headline says the page does not meet WCAG 2.1 Level AA, the reference benchmark (EN 301 549 V3.2.1), with the count of failures. With no failure it says the automated checks found none, that this is not a pass, and how many criteria are left for a person. Never "compliant" or "compliance score". Backed by a verified WCAG conformance-requirement fact. Amends 5 and 12. | Vojta, 2026-11-04: "the report should ideally clearly state whether its compliant or not". A failed SC instance is certain; a pass from automated checks is not. |
| 26 | 2026-11-04 | Requirement checklist. The report lists every requirement row: the 50 WCAG 2.1 A/AA criteria (the 6 WCAG 2.2 additions labelled newer) and the EAA lines beyond WCAG (Annex I Section III (b) and (d), Section IV (g), Art. 13 with Annex V), each with one status: fails, no failure found, judged by a model, needs a person, or not checked on this scan. Built from the catalogue, coverage and verified facts only. | Vojta, 2026-11-04: "use all the research and what every page needs to do". |
| 27 | 2026-11-04 | Accessibility-information check. Art. 13 and Annex V are researched and verified first. The scan looks for the site's accessibility statement through its links; Clef answers one question per Annex V point about the statement's text. The result is a judged observation in the EAA section, never a finding. | First check of an EAA obligation beyond WCAG; only a model can read a free-text statement against a list of points. |
| 28 | 2026-11-04 | Small crawl. Besides the page entered, the scan reads up to 4 more same-site pages picked deterministically from its links (a product page, contact, the accessibility statement, sign-in), each through `SafeFetch`. Static checks run on every page; the rendered session stays within the browser budget. Cross-page checks: 2.4.5 Multiple Ways, 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation, 3.2.4 Consistent Identification, 3.2.6 Consistent Help. The report stays the report for the page entered, with a section for the site. | Several criteria and the EAA lines cannot be decided from one page. |
| 29 | 2026-11-04 | More judged questions on evidence the scan collects: text styled as a heading but not marked up (1.3.1), instructions that rely on shape or position (1.3.3), passages in another language (3.1.2), form instructions (3.3.2), error messages after an empty submit with every network request blocked (3.3.1, 3.3.3), same function with the same name across pages (3.2.4), whether the site sells to consumers (EAA scope), and product accessibility information (IV (g)). Each is shown only after precision of at least 0.9 on at least 30 real items labelled by hand. Link purpose leaves out nav, header and footer links and dedupes by text. The scope judgement is shown beside the state list and never changes it. | Vojta, 2026-11-04: "use clef to somehow figure out if it does it correctly or not". Calibration is the cost; questions that miss the bar stay hidden. |
